WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.857
Relevant evidence can still be insufficient.

00:00:02.857 --> 00:00:07.392
Suppose the policy says returns are allowed within fourteen days.

00:00:07.392 --> 00:00:13.382
That is highly relevant to the question, can order one zero four two be returned?

00:00:13.382 --> 00:00:15.487
But it is not sufficient.

00:00:15.487 --> 00:00:18.204
We still need the authoritative delivery date.

00:00:18.664 --> 00:00:21.601
This distinction prevents a common rag mistake.

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:27.801
A system retrieves text on the right topic and assumes it has everything needed to answer.

00:00:27.801 --> 00:00:35.051
Ask two separate questions: does this evidence discuss the correct topic, and does it contain enough facts to support

00:00:35.051 --> 00:00:37.034
the actual decision?

00:00:37.597 --> 00:00:43.769
For multi-source projects, sufficiency is often more important than simple relevance.

00:00:43.769 --> 00:00:50.639
The workflow should know when it needs one more tool rather than trying to make the current evidence do more than it

00:00:50.639 --> 00:00:51.209
can.
